  "tldr": "Randomly formed groups behave unlike independent regression clusters once a positive fraction of eligible units is partitioned. The exact normalized Kneser operator yields a finite-sample design-variance identity and a dense limit in which only the degree-one Johnson projection of the arm difference survives. A two-permutation martingale then gives centered design-based Gaussian inference under predictable-variation stabilization. The familiar equal-group CR2 statistic consistently estimates the independent-group term, so it is valid but conservative in the dense regime and ratio-consistent exactly when the projection correction is negligible; when the sampling fraction vanishes, this recovers studentized normality without the birthday restriction \\(N_n^2/n\\to0\\). An enumerable eight-unit design verifies the factor-two variance excess. Exact one-realization variance recovery over the full dense class is posed honestly as an open question whose remaining step is a filtration-specific predictable-quadratic-variation and deterministic-diagonal argument for two equal-observation Rademacher priors.",
